## What each suite covers

| Suite | Covers |
|-------|--------|
| `00-health` | Server reachable, health endpoint, protected routes reject anonymous callers |
| `01-auth` | Sign-in as the seeded admin, session persistence, bad credentials, registration, sign-out |
| `02-dictionary` | Every ERD column registered in `sys_column`; form and grid field ordering |
| `03-crud.<entity>` | List, create, read, update (PATCH + PUT), version bump, validation rejection, 404s, sort, search, filter, soft delete |
| `04-bulk-seed` | {{config.recordsPerEntity}}+ faker records per entity, parents first; totals and pagination at volume |
| `05-rules.<entity>` | JDM built and validated, rule registered, valid records pass, invalid records prevented, dry-run has no side effects, deactivation stops enforcement |
| `06-rules-workflow` | `trigger-workflow` actions emitted and workflow runs enqueued, reaching a terminal state |
| `07-workflow-random` | Randomised creates/updates across random entities with cascade rules; no 5xx, no stuck runs, records stay readable |
| `08-users-roles` | Seeded roles, faker-registered users at volume, permission scoping, admin-only route enforcement |
| `10-benchmark` | Read latency at the volume the run built: first page, deep page, count, sort, filter, search |
| `11-performance-budget` | The same reads against ceilings, so a missing index fails rather than merely being slower |
| `12-concurrency` | 100 real threads writing to one entity at once — parallel inserts, updates and deletes, a contended row, and an audit of what the data looks like afterwards |

### The concurrency run

`12-concurrency` is the only suite that does not drive the application one
request at a time. It spawns `node:worker_threads` — a hundred by default, each
with its own session — and has them insert, update and delete against one
entity simultaneously, plus contend over a single shared record.

It measures throughput and tail latency, and those land in the run report next
to the single-threaded numbers. But the assertions are about correctness, which
is what concurrency actually threatens:

- no 5xx and no request that never answered — a pool that runs dry shows up here
- the contended record's version accounts for **every** accepted write to it,
  which is how a lost update is caught: two writers reading the same version and
  both writing the next one leaves the count short
- exactly one thread wins a conditional write when all hundred claim the same
  version — a check-and-set that reads the version without holding it lets
  several through
- every row a thread deleted is gone, and every row whose delete was refused
  because its cascade could not run is still whole

| Knob | Default | |
|---|---|---|
| `E2E_CONCURRENCY_WORKERS` | 100 | Threads |
| `E2E_CONCURRENCY_RECORDS` | 3 | Records each thread inserts, updates and deletes |
| `E2E_CONCURRENCY_CONTENDED` | 2 | Unconditional writes each thread aims at the shared record |
| `E2E_CONCURRENCY_ENTITY` | widest entity with no required parents | The table to hammer |
| `E2E_CONCURRENCY_MIN_OPS` | unset | Throughput floor to assert, when the hardware is known |

